  "observations": [
    "The protected source constructor accepts an opaque AssistantProviderContext and its exact applied receipt. Binding requires committed delivery for that same attempt/reference/runtime and derives the original Connection, generation, Participant, audience and correlation. A receipt from another instance is refused.",
    "Publication still requires current publisher session.observation.publish, plus .any when representing another identity. Merely possessing context_view does not grant publication. Reusing the same generic correlation cannot replace the provider-memory binding.",
    "Current source resolution reuses the existing frozen-context and cumulative-update validators: original root selection, every earlier exported update, original listener epochs and provider endpoint must remain authorized. It performs no nested authority acquisition inside the caller SQL snapshot.",
    "Both publication and observation-page projection take or reuse the complete current read interval. Missing/foreign/insufficient retained proofs never cause an authority upgrade or fallback. Ordinary composed reads forward their original interval.",
    "Generic publication is nonblocking when a writer has admission, avoiding a caller waiting on its own retained media reader. The protected native publication method can reuse the original AssistantProviderRead; the queued-writer regression also reads its page through that same interval and then releases it so the writer completes.",
    "Admitted provider cleanup hides queued derived content and stops publication while the native Connection remains independently current. Revoked history does the same without retiring that native source. A possible unacknowledged/uncertain update blocks observation use even though the initial context receipt remains delivered; an unused prepared update does not taint prior memory.",
    "The observation wire shape and HTTP route are unchanged. The new guards apply to the existing session.observations read path; logical IDs and original membership epochs remain stable. No compatibility, downgrade or external publication work was introduced."
  ],
